=========== J ===========
# Problem J — Equal Sum Segments
Time limit: 2s per test | Memory limit: 256 MB

## Statement
You are given an array $a$ of $n$ integers (elements may be negative).

A \emph{division} of the array is a partition of it into one or more contiguous segments such that every element belongs to exactly one segment. A division is called \emph{balanced} if all its segments have the same sum.

Count the number of balanced divisions. Two divisions are considered different if their sets of cut positions are different (a cut can be made between positions $i$ and $i+1$ for any $1 \le i < n$).

Note that the division into a single segment (the whole array, no cuts) is always balanced.

Since the number of divisions can be huge, print it modulo $10^9 + 7$.

## Input
The first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 10^5$) --- the number of test cases.

The first line of each test case contains a single integer $n$ ($1 \le n \le 10^5$) --- the size of the array.

The second line of each test case contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ ($-10^9 \le a_i \le 10^9$) --- the elements of the array.

It is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $10^5$.

## Output
For each test case, print a single integer --- the number of balanced divisions of the array, modulo $10^9 + 7$.

## Notes
The example contains six test cases.

\begin{itemize}
  \item $a = [1, 2, 1, 2]$, total sum $6$. Balanced divisions: the whole array (one segment of sum $6$), and $[1, 2]\;[1, 2]$ (two segments of sum $3$). Three segments of sum $2$ would require a prefix of sum $2$, which does not exist. Answer: $2$.
  \item $a = [1, -1, 1, -1]$, total sum $0$, so every segment must sum to $0$. The only position $i < n$ with prefix sum $0$ is $i = 2$, and we may either cut there or not. Answer: $2$.
  \item $a = [1, 1]$: $[1, 1]$ or $[1]\;[1]$. Answer: $2$.
  \item $a = [5]$: only the single segment. Answer: $1$.
  \item $a = [-1, -1]$: same as the third case with a negative total. Answer: $2$.
  \item $a = [1, 2, 3, 0, 2, 4]$, total sum $12$. With $k = 2$ segments (sum $6$ each) the cut can be made after index $3$ or after index $4$, since both prefix sums equal $6$ --- that gives $2$ divisions. No $k \in \{3, 4, 6\}$ works (e.g. for $k = 3$ a prefix of sum $4$ is required but absent). Together with the single-segment division, the answer is $3$.
\end{itemize}

## Example 1 — input
```
6
4
1 2 1 2
4
1 -1 1 -1
2
1 1
1
5
2
-1 -1
6
1 2 3 0 2 4
```
## Example 1 — output
```
2
2
2
1
2
3
```
=========== K ===========
# Problem K — Zero Segment
Time limit: 2s per test | Memory limit: 256 MB

## Statement
You are given an array $a$ of $n$ positive integers, and an integer $k$.

You may perform the following operations on the array any number of times, in any order:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Pay:} choose an index $i$ ($1 \le i \le n$), pay $a_i$ coins, and set $a_i := 0$.
  \item \textbf{Free:} choose three indices $l$, $m$, $r$ such that $1 \le l < m < r \le n$, and set $a_m := \max(a_l, a_r)$. This operation costs nothing.
\end{itemize}

Find the minimum total number of coins you have to pay so that some contiguous segment of length at least $k$ of the array consists only of zeros.

## Input
The first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 2 \cdot 10^5$) --- the number of test cases.

The first line of each test case contains two integers $n$ and $k$ ($1 \le k \le n \le 2 \cdot 10^5$) --- the size of the array and the minimum length of the required zero segment.

The second line of each test case contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ ($1 \le a_i \le 10^9$) --- the elements of the array.

It is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $2 \cdot 10^5$.

## Output
For each test case, print a single integer --- the minimum total cost needed to obtain a contiguous zero segment of length at least $k$.

## Notes
In the first test case of the first example, one way to spend exactly $3$ coins is as follows. Pay $1$ coin to set $a_1 := 0$. Apply the free operation with $(l, m, r) = (1, 2, 5)$: this sets $a_2 := \max(a_1, a_5) = \max(0, 5) = 5$ --- note that this does not help at all. Now pay $2$ coins to set $a_3 := 0$, and apply the free operation with $(l, m, r) = (1, 2, 3)$ to set $a_2 := \max(a_1, a_3) = 0$. The segment $[1, 3]$ is all zeros. Trying to zero a longer segment, say $[2, 4]$, turns out to cost more, and careful consideration of every possibility shows that cost $3$ cannot be beaten.

In the second test case of the first example the answer is $40$: a suitable sequence of operations achieves it, and one can check that no cheaper sequence exists.

In the third test case of the first example, $k = 1$, so after paying $7$ coins for the only element the array already contains a zero segment of length $1$.

In the second example, for the first test case the cheapest single payment has cost $1$, which is sufficient. For the second test case, the optimal cost is $9$.

## Example 1 — input
```
4
5 3
1 4 2 3 5
3 3
10 20 30
1 1
7
4 2
5 1 1 5
```
## Example 1 — output
```
3
40
7
2
```

## Example 2 — input
```
2
6 1
9 3 8 1 10 2
5 4
8 2 7 1 9
```
## Example 2 — output
```
1
9
```
=========== L ===========
# Problem L — The Coral Loop
Time limit: 2s per test | Memory limit: 256 MB

## Statement
The Coral Loop is a ring of $n$ tiny islands, numbered from $1$ to $n$ around the atoll. Causeway $i$ connects island $i$ with island $i+1$ (for $1 \le i \le n-1$), and causeway $n$ loops back from island $n$ to island $1$. Crossing causeway $i$ costs $a_i$ shells --- several causeways cross such gorgeous lagoons that the paddlers' cooperative pays \emph{you} a rebate to use them, so some $a_i$ are negative.

In the middle of the atoll floats the Ferry Nook, a seaplane dock. A flight between the Nook and island $i$ costs $b_i$ shells, promos and rebates included, so some $b_i$ are negative as well. All causeways and flights operate in both directions at the same price.

Lani runs the trip kiosk. By the Paddlers' Charter --- twice-paddled water is wasted daylight --- a valid route may visit each location (islands and the Nook) at most once. Over the season she logs $q$ events of three kinds:

\begin{itemize}
\item A traveler asks for the cheapest valid route from location $s$ to location $t$ ($s < t$);
\item Causeway $x$ is repriced: $a_x$ becomes $w$;
\item The flight between the Nook and island $x$ is repriced: $b_x$ becomes $w$.
\end{itemize}

After every traveler's question Lani must announce the best possible price. The season is long, so help her answer quickly.

## Input
The first line contains a single integer $t$ ($1 \le t \le 3 \cdot 10^5$) --- the number of test cases.

The first line of each test case contains two integers $n$ and $q$ ($2 \le n \le 3 \cdot 10^5$, $1 \le q \le 3 \cdot 10^5$) --- the number of islands in the Coral Loop and the number of logged events.

The second line contains $n$ integers $a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n$ ($-10^9 \le a_i \le 10^9$) --- the causeway prices: causeway $i$ connects islands $i$ and $i \bmod n + 1$.

The third line contains $n$ integers $b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n$ ($-10^9 \le b_i \le 10^9$) --- the prices of flights between island $i$ and the Nook.

Each of the next $q$ lines describes one event in one of the following formats:
\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{1 s t} ($1 \le s < t \le n+1$, where location $n+1$ is the Nook) --- print the cheapest valid route from location $s$ to location $t$;
\item \texttt{2 x w} ($1 \le x \le n$, $-10^9 \le w \le 10^9$) --- set $a_x := w$;
\item \texttt{3 x w} ($1 \le x \le n$, $-10^9 \le w \le 10^9$) --- set $b_x := w$.
\end{itemize}

It is guaranteed that the sum of $n$ over all test cases does not exceed $3 \cdot 10^5$, and the sum of $q$ over all test cases does not exceed $3 \cdot 10^5$.

## Output
For every event of type $1$, print a single integer on its own line --- the minimum possible price of a route from $s$ to $t$ that visits no location twice. Prices may be negative: rebates can make the cooperative pay the traveler.

## Notes
In the example the atoll has $n = 3$ islands with causeway prices $a = (4, -2, 5)$ and flight prices $b = (3, 7, 1)$.

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{1 1 3}: paddling $1 \to 2 \to 3$ costs $4 - 2 = 2$, causeway $3$ directly costs $5$, and flying through the Nook costs $3 + 1 = 4$. The answer is $2$.
\item \texttt{2 2 6}: the rebate lagoon causeway is repriced, $a_2 := 6$.
\item \texttt{1 1 3}: now paddling $1 \to 2 \to 3$ costs $10$, so the Nook route ($3 + 1 = 4$) wins.
\item \texttt{3 1 -4}: flight to island $1$ goes on promo, $b_1 := -4$.
\item \texttt{1 1 3}: flying $1 \to \text{Nook} \to 3$ costs $-4 + 1 = -3$ --- the cooperative pays the traveler $3$ shells.
\item \texttt{1 1 4}: location $4$ is the Nook; the direct flight from island $1$ costs $-4$, and nothing beats a promo.
\end{itemize}

## Example 1 — input
```
1
3 6
4 -2 5
3 7 1
1 1 3
2 2 6
1 1 3
3 1 -4
1 1 3
1 1 4
```
## Example 1 — output
```
2
4
-3
-4
```
